-- title:
--   Kernel of an Atkin–Lehner-pinned reduction map, mod p
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, a nonzero level $M$ with $p \mid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbf{Z}/M)^\times$ such that every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbf{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbf{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial already lies in $H$. Let $W$ be an Atkin–Lehner datum for $(M,p)$, that is, an integer $R$ with $M = pR$ together with $a,b \in \mathbf{Z}$ satisfying $pa - Rb = 1$, let $e \in (\mathbf{Z}/M)^\times$, and let $K$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbf{Z}/p$-algebra structure. Let $\rho$ be a $K$-linear map from $K \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}/p} \mathtt{IntTwoCuspForms}\,M\,H\,p$ — the base change of the reduction of the two-cusp lattice of weight-$2$ cusp forms on $\Gamma_H(M)$ modulo the ideal `intIdeal p` — to the module $\Omega$ of Kähler differentials over $K$ of the subfield of $K(\!(q)\!)$ generated by the integral form ratios attached to $\Gamma_{H'}(M/p)$, where $H'$ is the image of $H$ in $(\mathbf{Z}/(M/p))^\times$. Assume the Atkin–Lehner pinning hypothesis: for every weight-$2$ cusp form $f$ on $\Gamma_H(M)$ lying in the two-cusp integral set for $p$ over the prime subring of $\mathbf{C}$ (all $q$-coefficients of $tf$ and of $(tf)\mid W'$ are rational integers, for every element $t$ of the Hecke ring `heckeRingH M H 2` and every Atkin–Lehner datum $W'$ at $(M,p)$), and every power series $p_{fW}$ over $\mathbf{Z}$ whose image in $\mathbf{C}[\![q]\!]$ is the $q$-expansion of $\mathrm{alSlash}\,W\,2\,(\langle e \rangle f)$ (with $\langle e \rangle$ the diamond operator `diamondLinH 2 e`, which is $0$ unless `StableD M H 2` holds), the $q$-expansion `diffQExp` of $\rho(1 \otimes \overline{f})$ equals $p_{fW}$ read as a Laurent series over $K$. Then every $x$ in the source with $\rho x = 0$ lies in the $K$-span of the elements $1 \otimes \overline{y}$, where $y$ runs over the two-cusp lattice and all $q$-coefficients of $\mathrm{alSlash}\,W\,2\,(\langle e \rangle y)$ are $p$ times a rational integer.
--
--   This is the Atkin–Lehner counterpart of the corresponding statement at the cusp $\infty$: it identifies the kernel of a reduction map to differentials on the lower-level modular curve as being spanned by the classes of integral forms whose Fourier coefficients at the Atkin–Lehner cusp, read through the diamond operator $\langle e \rangle$, are all divisible by $p$. It is used in [`ModularCurve.eq_zero_of_isInfReductionMap_apply_eq_zero_of_apply_eq_zero_alSlash`](thm.html#ModularCurve.eq_zero_of_isInfReductionMap_apply_eq_zero_of_apply_eq_zero_alSlash), within the comparison of mod-$p$ cusp forms of level $M$ with differentials in level $M/p$ that underlies level lowering.
